"JH" <no@email> wrote in news:vg459g99brs691@corp.supernews.com:

> Pardon me if this is the wrong place to ask, but here's the situation.
>  My brother and I play nethack on separate machines on our home
> network.  I was trying to think of a way to share bones files between
> the two computers.  My first idea was a simple batch file that would 
> copy the bones from a shared folder before playing, and copy them back
> after playing.  However, depending on the order of events, (i.e. if we
> were playing at the same time and ended at different times) some bones
> information would be lost.  At least, that's my understanding, it
> looks like the bones for all levels in a branch are all stored in the
> same file.  So is there any way to easily combine bones file 
> information? I don't know if I want to use hearse, just because our
> internet connection is fairly slow, so I'd prefer to just share over
> the network. This is on a pair of windows platforms, using the
> nethackw.exe binary, if that makes any difference
> 
I don't think Hearse uses an awful lot of bandwidth, it should be fine on a 
slow connection.

Otherwise, yes you can just copy the individual files around if you want. 
You could write something that randomly mixed up the existing bones files 
between directories when you started playing. That way sometimes you would 
get your own and sometimes your brother's bones.

Each bones file just contains one bones level, so as long as you don't 
overwrite anything you should be ok. Use a scripting language such as 
Python or Perl to grab a list of bones files, find pairs with the same 
filename and randomly either swap the files or leave them alone, then 
randomly allocate a directory for each of the other files.

Or a simpler method you could do by batch file would be to swap all the 
bones files every time you start a new game. Move bones from A to tmp, B to 
A, tmp to B. That should gradually result in a mix of bones, and I think 
ought to be safe even if you both run it simultaneously, or if Nethack 
tries to read or write a bones file as you are trying to move it (I think 
it should just give an error message and continue):

(untested:)
    @echo off
    SET remote=D:\Games\Nethack\Bones
    SET local=C:\Games\Nethack\Bones
    mkdir %TMP%\Bones 2>nul
    for %%f in (%remote%\Bon*.*) MOVE %%f %TMP%\Bones
    for %%f in (%local%\Bon*.*) MOVE %%f %remote%
    MOVE %TMP%\Bones\Bon*.* %local%
    NETHACK %*

